Connect signed current to accumulated and stored charge (@physics-charge-flow: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "../electromagnetism/foundations/Charge-conservation-and-current-density.lingua#r_16QY5HH6CZN53EJ3J1AERMB3TA") { r_00A2CB2AB667C70FE4724DA940
Define a cumulative signed charge S(t) crossing an oriented cut, measured relative to a stated initial time. When differentiable, I(t)=dS/dt. The charge crossing from t₀ to t₁ is ∫I dt. An ampere is a coulomb per second. Current is a rate through a specified cut, not the amount stored near it, a particle speed, or energy per time. Negative carriers moving opposite the chosen positive direction contribute positive conventional current. Multiple carrier populations can contribute to the same net rate.

For a fixed region, Q′=ΣI_in−ΣI_out when the selected cuts account for every charge crossing. This is the finite boundary version of charge conservation. The familiar zero sum at a junction follows only when its changing net stored charge is negligible at the scale of interest. If 3 μA enters and 1 μA leaves a region for 2 ms, its net charge increases by 4 nC. Setting the sum to zero would discard the stated accumulation. Choose enough time resolution to justify any neglect.

In a capacitor model, conduction current arrives at one plate and leaves the other while their charges separate. The ideal insulating gap need not carry the same conduction current as the metal leads. Treating each plate as a separate boundary gives Q′=I with the correct orientation; treating the whole neutral two-plate device gives a different net-charge account. Later magnetic-field consistency introduces displacement current. This common step establishes the charge ledger without claiming to derive the complete field theory.

At a circuit port choose v=V_a−V_b and a current i entering terminal a and leaving b. In the lumped approximation with no omitted energy channel, the electrical power absorbed at that port is p=vi. To see the sign on a resistive path, a positive increment dq passed from a to b loses potential energy (V_a−V_b)dq, transferred to other energy channels. Division by time gives the port rate. For an ideal capacitor the same terminal-work account describes separation of plate charge, not carriers crossing its dielectric; the next storage lesson derives that case explicitly. A negative p means the port delivers electrical energy. Negative carrier motion is already represented by signed current; it does not require changing this convention a second time.

Energy is ∫vi dt, not generally the product of separate averages times duration. For example, during two equal one-second intervals let (v,i) be (2 V,1 A) and (0 V,0 A). The absorbed energy is 2 J; multiplying average voltage 1 V by average current 1/2 A and total time 2 s gives only 1 J. The missing information is their time pairing. Connect this to the earlier inquiry distinction between separate averages and dependence.

**Work:** draw the directions for a source delivering 5 V at 1 mA and a passive element absorbing its output. Report each signed port power and the energy over 3 s. Reconstruct the junction example with reversed cut orientations. Make distinct ledgers for capacitor plate charge, total device charge and port energy. Read [University Physics Volume 2 — OpenStax](../electromagnetism/sources/University-Physics-Volume-2.lingua#r_5JQ0CPQ0RNJNVH175Y9V305J5Q) 9.1 and selected 9.5 power definitions, then the bounded common part of [Charge conservation and current density](../electromagnetism/foundations/Charge-conservation-and-current-density.lingua#r_16QY5HH6CZN53EJ3J1AERMB3TA). Its local density/divergence equations remain an introduction to later vector calculus, not formulas already derived by this finite ledger.

**Continue:** [Charge conservation and current density](../electromagnetism/foundations/Charge-conservation-and-current-density.lingua#r_16QY5HH6CZN53EJ3J1AERMB3TA).

